generated_solution:
Since we're looking for the perimeter of the triangle, we need to express the area as a function of the side length.
According to Wikipedia the formula for the area of an equilateral triangle is:
\begin{equation}
area = \frac{side^2 \cdot \sqrt{3}}{4}.
\end{equation}
So we can set up the following system of equations and solve for the side length:
\begin{align}
side^2 \cdot \sqrt{3}/4 &= side\\
side^2 \cdot \sqrt{3} &= 4 \cdot side\\
side \cdot \sqrt{3} &= 4\\
side &= 4 / \sqrt{3}
\end{align}
Now, we can calculate the perimeter of the triangle:
$$
perimeter = 3 \cdot side = 3 \cdot \frac{4}{\sqrt{3}} = \boxed{\frac{12}{\sqrt{3}}}
$$

