Let us denote the inverse of $f$ as $g$. Then, we have:
\begin{align*}
g(4) &= 3\\
g(1) &= 5\\
g(5) &= 2
\end{align*}
Now, we can evaluate $f^{-1}(5)+f^{-1}(4)$.
\begin{align*}
f^{-1}(5)+f^{-1}(4) &= g(5)+g(4)\\
&= 2+3\\
&= 5
\end{align*}
Since $f$ is its own inverse, we have:
\begin{align*}
f^{-1}\left(f^{-1}(5)+f^{-1}(4)\right) &= f^{-1}(5)\\
&= 2
\end{align*}
Therefore, the value of $f^{-1}\left(f^{-1}(5)+f^{-1}(4)\right)$ is $2$.

So the answer is \boxed{2}.

